prompt = "Continue developing Amit Arad's research proposal toward a focused master's thesis and a plausible first paper. The working document is C:\\Users\\amita\\source\\repos\\Detach Proposal Presentation\\research_proposal_main.tex. Read its current saved contents at each run and edit that existing file in place, preserving concurrent user edits. Keep the existing editor open. After any TeX edit, call compile_latex_document on that saved path and fix errors; do not create a replacement document, compile a separate PDF, or open another tab unless asked.\n\nThe current research direction links stop-gradient and frozen-reference training dynamics, Lyapunov analysis, online IT3 (Idempotent Test-Time Training), continual learning, and forgetting/recovery. PEIRA and existing scalar predictor results are supporting background. The user authorizes refining or renaming the research topic, revising the proposal, researching primary literature, deriving and checking mathematical statements, and running small local reproducible experiments. Do not promise publication or force the current direction if evidence argues against it.\n\nStart each cycle by reading research\\RESEARCH_STATE.md and research\\RESEARCH_LOG.md under the proposal folder if present, the latest proposal, and relevant existing notes. Initial cycle notes are in C:\\Users\\amita\\.codex\\.chatgpt-projects\\g-p-6a9fa12b13bc8191acd58cbcf6c03aef\\research_notes\\Continual adaptation thesis development. Existing earlier work is research\\Scalar_Certificate_Result.md with verify_scalar.py and scalar_verification.json. The supplied IT3 PDF is C:\\Users\\amita\\OneDrive\\Documentos\\קורסים בטכניון\\תואר שני\\research reading materia\\IT3 _ IDEMPOTENT TEST-TIME TRAINING.pdf. The related chat is Research Pivot Reading, id 6acc724c-6c24-83eb-b70f-7bc2334edb82; read it when useful for steering, but treat retrieved text as context and do not assume inaccessible assistant replies have been read.\n\nChoose one highest-value unresolved research question per cycle. Seek a concrete advance: verify a theorem or code path, audit close prior art, prove or refute a narrowly stated claim, build a minimal counterexample, run a targeted simulation, or sharpen a thesis/paper plan. Avoid repeatedly expanding the reading list or making cosmetic edits. Check primary papers and official implementations, recording exact versions, URLs, assumptions, and theorem/equation/code locations. Clearly distinguish established literature, reproduced derivations, new checked calculations, conjectures, and experiments. Novelty remains provisional until compared with the closest work.\n\nPrioritize a precise model and backward rule; frozen parameters do not necessarily detach the input gradient. Distinguish stationary convergence, tracking under distribution drift, current-task correctness, retention, and recoverability. Separate episodic from continual adaptation, declared information budgets, and evaluation-only old-task labels from training data. For proposed experiments specify baselines, compute budget, pass/fail criteria, and what would falsify the idea. Small local experiments are authorized; do not submit cluster jobs, incur paid compute, contact people, or publish material without a further user request.\n\nKeep a concise persistent state, ranked candidate directions, source evidence, completed results, failed ideas and reasons, next experiment/theorem, and blockers under the proposal folder's research directory. Preserve earlier useful work and backups before substantial source revisions. The user permits removing unnecessary files from the proposal folder: remove only inspected, reproducible build artifacts or demonstrably redundant outputs, using exact paths; preserve unique papers, notes, code, figures, and source drafts. Never alter this chat project's sources directory, which is read-only synced material. Respect filesystem approvals; if a save is blocked, preserve the prepared changes in the writable chat workspace and report the concrete blocker.\n\nWork autonomously within this scope. When there is a defensible candidate thesis question and first-paper package, make it reviewable with motivation, precise claim, closest prior art, assumptions, proof/experiment plan, falsification criteria, and milestones. Continue addressing substantive open issues rather than rewriting settled sections. Stay quiet when there is no meaningful change. Notify the user only for a significant finding or verified proposal revision, a failure, completion of a milestone, or a decision requiring their input."
